        {
          "headline": "Survey of 1,185 US adults: 63.1% would wait up to 48 hours for clinician review of a medical portal question",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "JAMA Network Open", "url": "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2855284" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "In a cross-sectional online survey fielded June 17 to July 26, 2024, participants chose between a fast answer from the AI within 5 minutes and waiting up to 48 hours for a health professional to review it: 748 (63.1%) preferred to wait for clinician review of medical messages, while 680 (57.4%) preferred the faster AI answer for logistical messages and 600 (50.6%) for general messages (P < .001).",
            "For mental-health support, 757 (63.9%) said they were most likely to use a human therapist available in 3 months, against 595 (50.2%) for therapist-monitored AI available in 1 month and 525 (44.3%) for AI alone available immediately.",
            "Women were less likely than men to accept AI for medical portal messages (prevalence ratio 0.76, 95% CI 0.65–0.90), and Black or African American participants were more likely than White participants (PR 1.40, 95% CI 1.20–1.64).",
            "The research letter was published online October 14, 2026. Of 1,237 who completed the survey, 52 (4.2%) were excluded for failed attention checks. The authors note response time is confounded with modality, the vignettes are hypothetical, and the sample is nonprobability."
          ],
          "topics": ["healthcare", "public-opinion"],
          "impact": "neutral",
          "flags": ["single-source"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Transformer trained on 7.4 million Austrian inpatients lifts in-hospital death prediction from 0.866 to 0.879 AUC",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "npj Digital Medicine", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-026-03265-2" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "Using 13 years of Austrian inpatient data covering 7.4 million patients, the authors trained a visit-level contrastive transformer to encode diagnosis sequences and inter-admission timing into patient-history embeddings. In a downstream cohort of 1.7 million individuals it improved prediction of in-hospital death over an Elixhauser-based comorbidity model, with an AUC of 0.879 versus 0.866 (AUC gain 0.0132, 95% CI 0.0118–0.0144).",
            "Across 130 incident ICD-10 disease-block outcomes the embedding model had higher AUC for 93, with what the authors call a modest median gain of 0.006, concentrated in mental, musculoskeletal, nervous system and metabolic disorders.",
            "The sharper result is in stratification: among patients with similar age, sex and comorbidity-model risk, those assigned high residual risk had 127–181 fewer event-free days over five years, and event rates comparable to low-residual-risk patients more than a decade older. For event-free survival overall the margin was narrow, AUC 0.726 against 0.722."
          ],
          "topics": ["healthcare", "ai-for-science"],
          "impact": "neutral",
          "flags": ["single-source"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Federation of State Medical Boards convenes a 22-member workgroup on AI that practises with little physician supervision",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "STAT News", "url": "https://www.statnews.com/2026/10/14/lobbyists-trade-groups-ama-push-autonomous-clinical-ai-regulations/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "STAT reports that a 22-member AI workgroup convened by the Federation of State Medical Boards includes executives from the American Medical Association, the American Hospital Association, Anthropic and the insurer Elevance Health, and that lobbying groups, a health insurer, technology companies and state-government representatives met behind closed doors in Washington, D.C., in early October.",
            "FSMB president and CEO Humayun Chaudhry told STAT that particular attention is going \"to AI tools that perform clinical functions with limited or no direct physician supervision\", and that a draft could be made public in \"weeks to months\". An FSMB spokesman said the workgroup \"was built to balance expertise across medical regulation, AI development, care delivery, academic medicine, state AI policy, and patient safety\".",
            "Because states individually regulate medical licensure and the practice of medicine, STAT reports the recommendations could become the basis of state legislation. The workgroup's discussions are confidential and no draft text is public; the recommendations are to be opened for feedback before formal adoption."
          ],
          "topics": ["healthcare", "us-state-policy", "anthropic"],
          "impact": "neutral",
