        {
          "headline": "Financial Stability Board finalises 12 sound practices for AI adoption at financial institutions",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Financial Stability Board", "url": "https://www.fsb.org/2026/10/fsb-finalises-sound-practices-for-the-responsible-adoption-of-artificial-intelligence-ai/" },
            { "name": "Financial Stability Board", "url": "https://www.fsb.org/2026/10/sound-practices-for-responsible-adoption-of-artificial-intelligence-ai-final-report/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "The FSB's final report sets out 12 sound practices in two groups: four on organisation-wide AI governance — board and senior management oversight, a governance structure with defined roles, integrating AI risk into risk management, and continuous learning and adaptation — and eight on managing risk across the AI lifecycle, from use-case assessment through to third-party AI dependencies.",
            "The FSB says it \"strongly encourages the board and senior management of financial institutions to reference the sound practices\" when developing their AI governance, risk management and control frameworks, and describes the set as a practical toolkit institutions can tailor to their context and risk profile.",
            "The report reflects public feedback on a consultative version published in June 2026, and the FSB published an overview of consultation responses the same day. The practices are not binding, and the FSB says it will monitor developments and update them as necessary."
          ],
          "topics": ["financial-regulation", "agents"],
          "impact": "neutral"
        },
        {
          "headline": "UK officials tell MPs that AI safeguards in the Cyber Security and Resilience Bill are still only under consideration",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "The Register", "url": "https://www.theregister.com/ai-and-ml/2026/10/15/uk-keeps-ai-safeguards-in-the-maybe-pile-for-cyber-security-bill/5303026" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "The Register reports Alexandra Jones, director general at the Department for Business, Innovation, Science and Trade, told MPs the government proposes using the Cyber Security and Resilience (Network and Information Systems) Bill to improve preparedness for AI, cyber and loss-of-control risks — including how serious incidents and near misses are reported and how AI-driven disruption is contained — and that it is \"developing the policy to look at whether we use the Cyber Bill to do that\". No amendments are committed.",
            "Committee chair Liam Byrne replied: \"You can't consider something in a bill. You have to legislate for something in a bill.\" AI minister Kanishka Narayan said this week the government would step up national defences against growing AI risks, adding: \"We will legislate wherever necessary.\"",
            "Daniel Kokotajlo of the AI Futures Project told the committee his group's \"least bad plan\" included no recursive self-improvement, more industry transparency and more regulation; Byrne summarised the testimony as saying companies cannot be trusted to self-regulate. Responsibility for the bill moved to the Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport after recent machinery-of-government changes."
          ],
          "topics": ["uk", "cyber-defense", "alignment"],
          "impact": "neutral",
          "flags": ["single-source"]
        }
      ]
